const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Does process automation mean we no longer need people for compliance work?',
    a: 'No. Automation takes over the repetitive operations — collecting data, checking formats, turning that data into a report, tracking deadlines. What stays with people is exactly what requires judgement: the decision, the relationship with the supplier, the interpretation of an unusual case and the signature. A well-built automated process reduces manual workload and moves attention to exceptions.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long until we see a result?',
    a: 'It depends on your data volume and on how consistent your inputs are today. The first reporting cycle is usually the most expensive one, because it also includes setting up the flow. The gain becomes visible in the second and third cycles, when data is reused instead of rebuilt from scratch. That is why the assessment is made over 12 months, not over the first month.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do we have to give up our ERP or our current systems?',
    a: 'No. In a compliance process, existing systems remain the source of truth for operational data. What gets added on top is validation, traceability and reporting. The most expensive evaluation mistake is starting from the premise that all data has to be moved into a new tool before it can produce any value.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What do we do when supplier data arrives in different formats?',
    a: 'That is the rule, not the exception. The flow is built with normalisation and validation rules, and whatever cannot be normalised automatically becomes an explicit task for a person, with the reason and the deadline displayed. The difference from manual work is not that the problems disappear, but that they become visible before the reporting deadline.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do we estimate ROI if we have no measurements from before?',
    a: 'They can be reconstructed. Take the last two or three completed cycles and measure them retroactively: hours spent per report, the number of returns or corrections, the delays against the deadline and what they cost. An approximate but documented baseline is enough to compare offers — and far more useful than an industry average that has nothing to do with your volume.',
  },
];
